WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Prisma schema design is not only a developer-experience concern.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:11.880
The schema describes models, relationships, field names, generated client behavior, and migrations.

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:18.140
It shapes what the application can query, update, expose, and evolve over time.

4
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:39.760
A model that contains tenantId, role, permission, ownerId, accountStatus, passwordHash, tokenHash, resetToken, emailVerified, isAdmin, deletedAt, createdBy, updatedBy, or audit fields is carrying security meaning, even if those fields look ordinary in the schema file.

5
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.080
Schema constraints can support integrity.

6
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:50.700
Unique indexes, required fields, relations, defaults, and database constraints can prevent some inconsistent states.

7
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.040
They do not replace authorization.

8
00:00:53.140 --> 00:01:01.380
A relation may show that a record belongs to a user, but application code still has to enforce that the current caller may act on that relationship.

9
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:09.800
A required tenantId may support tenant scoping, but the server still must decide which tenant context is trusted and where it is applied.

10
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.720
Avoid exposing internal model structure directly as public API structure.

11
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:22.420
Generated types can make it tempting to pass large objects into create or update calls because the TypeScript compiler accepts them.

12
00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.360
That does not mean the caller should be allowed to set every field.

13
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:34.380
Sensitive fields need clear rules for who may read them, write them, filter on them, include them in responses, or change them during migrations.

14
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:42.720
Schema changes should be reviewed as security-relevant when they affect identity, ownership, permissions, tenant boundaries, or sensitive data.
